    "section": "Europe",
    "title": "UKGC makes limit-setting, session data and staff alerts mandatory on new gaming machines",
    "meta": "iGaming Business | 1 Oct 2026",
    "summary": "The Gambling Commission has confirmed new Gaming Machine Technical Standards for new category A, B and some C machines. At the start of each session, players must either set their own time and loss limits or accept default ones. Consumer-set limits can go up to 60 minutes and £450 net loss. Defaults are capped at 20 minutes and £150, and playing with no limits is not allowed. The rules come in three stages: a session definition on 30 March 2027, most of the changes on 30 June 2027, and staff alerts becoming a legal duty on 3 October 2028.",
    "why": "These rules apply to land-based machines, but they set out in detail how the UKGC now expects limit prompts, net-position displays and mandatory breaks to work. Rules for remote gambling could follow the same model. Teams building RG modules and session engines should treat the logic here as a template for future online requirements.",
    "expanded": "Machines must show net position and time played by default, turbo and quick-spin features are banned, and machines must not play 'celebrations' for returns at or below the stake. The Commission estimates staff-alert costs at £20.76m–£41.63m a year, mostly for data systems and extra staff. Helen Rhodes said the measures will 'empower consumers to understand and manage their gambling and ensure operators play their part.' Platforms that already run real-time limit and alert workflows will be well placed if similar mandates reach online play.",
