    "title": "Bettormetrics US Open data: duration-based sportsbook uptime hides outages when betting peaks",
    "meta": "iGB | 29 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Bettormetrics analysed $64m in exchange wagering across 39 in-play US Open men's singles matches and 18 operators. It compared conventional uptime, measured as a share of match time, with uptime weighted by when money was actually being wagered. LeoVegas posted 97.8% uptime by duration but only 88.3% weighted by wagering, the lowest in the sample. In Zverev vs Tabilo it was available for 98.5% of the match but only for periods covering 61.5% of exchange wagering.",
    "why": "SLA and uptime reporting based on match duration can hide outages that cost revenue during peak betting periods. Sportsbook platform providers should consider adding wagering-weighted availability to client dashboards and SLAs, and should load-test suspension logic against high-volume moments such as tie-breaks.",
    "expanded": "Pinnacle had the tightest overround at 3.7% and modest theoretical arbitrage of 4.8% by duration. DraftKings, at 4.5% overround, carried 14.9% arbitrage, roughly three times as much. Bet365 was in theoretical arbitrage for 56.5% of the Navone vs Djokovic match. Bettormetrics says it will add prediction-market data next, which would let this weighted benchmarking extend to the NFL and NBA.",
