    "title": "Steve Ruddock: banning some micro-bets would make match-fixing easier to police",
    "meta": "Straight to the Point (Steve Ruddock) | 30 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "At G2E, New Jersey Assistant AG Louis Rogacki argued that banning micro-betting would not solve match-fixing on its own. Panellists including the NFL, Genius Sports and BetMGM said a regulated framework with integrity monitoring is what catches fixes. Steve Ruddock responded in his Straight to the Point newsletter, citing player-prop cases involving Jontay Porter, Terry Rozier, Emmanuel Clase and Luis Ortiz, and Malik Beasley.",
    "why": "Pressure on player props and micro-markets is rising from the NCAA, regulators and integrity bodies. Market-level controls are becoming a likely regulatory lever, and platforms that cannot restrict prop markets at a fine-grained level risk losing whole market categories.",
    "expanded": "Ruddock argues for targeted limits rather than outright bans. He notes that prop-based fixes do not require changing team outcomes, so suspicious betting activity is often the only signal. Platforms should combine market controls with per-market integrity alert feeds.",
    "commentary": "Ruddock accepts that the regulated market catches fixes. But he argues that 'banning easy-to-manipulate markets, which some micro-bets clearly are, would in fact make policing match-fixing much easier.' He does not call for outright bans. Instead he repeats his earlier view that the right fix is to 'turn player props into entertainment markets and limit bets on college player props to an amount that would make match-fixing silly.' His central point is that 'this isn't a game integrity problem, it's a betting integrity problem'. Isolated player stats can be manipulated by one person, with information as small as a planned early exit or a nagging injury, and without changing the team's result.",
    "takeaway": "Product teams should build market-level controls into the sportsbook: per-market stake caps, separate limits for college and player-prop markets, and the ability to downgrade markets to low-limit 'entertainment' status by jurisdiction. Being able to apply limits like these quickly is a better position with regulators than switching off whole prop categories.",
